lib/File/SOPS.pm, in the `rotate` POD (around line 1728 at f9c2f8b), says:

    ... so C<< unencrypted_regex => '^\w+$' >> classifies a key with a
    non-ASCII character differently in the two implementations, and sops
    encrypts a value this library then thought was never encrypted. That
    divergence is open and is karr #161; this refusal is what stops it
    costing a secret in the meantime.

karr #161 closed it (docs/adr/0048): the two rule patterns are compiled /a, and
that exact example now classifies the same way on both sides. The paragraph is
now wrong on its own terms, and the sentence after it -- "this refusal is what
stops it costing a secret in the meantime" -- describes a guard that no longer
has that job for this case.

Also worth revisiting in the same pass: t/60's last interop subtest was
rewritten for the same reason and now pins that rotate goes THROUGH.

Not fixed with karr #161 because lib/File/SOPS.pm was being changed by the INI
lane (karr #37) at the time and was outside that change's file boundary.

